<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giresun</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kerasous, Cerasus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giresun, the ancient Kerasous (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerasus">Cerasus</a>), is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Founded as a Greek colony of Sinope, it came under the kings of Pontus in the second century BCE and later belonged to the Roman and Byzantine empires and the Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>, before the Ottoman conquest in the fifteenth century. It is now the capital of Giresun Province.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giresun.jpg" alt="Giresun" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mkadioglu2020 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giresun_Kalesinden_Giresun-Do%C4%9Fu_Taraf%C4%B1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Giresun lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, about 175 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a>. The older parts of the city stand on a peninsula crowned by a ruined Byzantine fortress, which shelters a small natural harbour. The ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> called the place Kerasous, and the modern name is derived from it.</p><p>The name is probably from a pre-Greek word for the cherry, and Pliny says Lucullus first exported the cherry from Kerasous to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in Roman times. Another theory derives it from the Greek keras, horn, because of the horn-shaped peninsula. The Turks made it Giresun after they gained permanent control of the region in the late 14th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharnaces-i-of-pontus">Pharnaces I of Pontus</a> renamed the city Pharnacia after himself, and it was called that as late as the 2nd century CE. According to A. H. M. Jones, it officially reverted to Kerasous in 64 CE. Xenophon names Kerasous in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabasis">Anabasis</a>.</p><p>In late antiquity it became a Christian bishopric, and the bishops Gregorius at the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> in 431 and Gratianus at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon">Council of Chalcedon</a> in 451 are among those recorded. It remained a metropolitanate until 1703, when its church was placed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a>. In the Empire of Trebizond it was the second city. Alexios II Megas Komnenos defeated the Turkoman Koustoganes there in September 1302 and built a fortress overlooking the sea.</p><p>From 1244 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> moved into the area, at times pursued by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Chalybia held the city briefly from 1398 to 1400. By the 15th century most of the coast belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>. Local tradition says that Kerasous held out for many months after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> in 1461 and then surrendered on terms that let the Christian inhabitants keep their arms.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> of the island of Aretias held out against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> for seven years. Early in the 20th century the city had 14,000 residents, of whom 1,200 families were Greek, 1,000 Turkish and 300 Armenian. After the Pontic Greek genocide the surviving Greeks were expelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in the 1923 population exchange.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Giresun is the seat of Giresun Province and has a population of 125,682. The surrounding region grows most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>'s hazelnuts, as well as walnuts and cherries, and the port has long handled these products. A well-preserved castle stands in the city centre. Giresun Island, called Aretias in antiquity, is the place where, according to Apollonius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argonauts">Argonauts</a> met the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazons</a>, who had dedicated a temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ares">Ares</a>. A fertility festival is held there each May. The old Ottoman houses of the Zeytinlik district survive.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Giresun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>What remained was a narrow Greek state along the coast between roughly <b>Kerasous</b> and the borders of Georgia, protected on its landward side by the Pontic Mountains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
